mbedded INSIDE ChatGPT.app. That's part of the ChatGPT desktop app (prod=ChatGPT_Mac). So \"uninstalling the Codex kernel\" isn't a separate app — it's part of ChatGPT.app.\n- `~/Library/Application Support/com.openai.codex` — hmm, wait. Actually, is there a separate official Codex desktop app? Let me check: `~/Library/Application Support/Codex` and `com.openai.codex`. The crashpad database was `/Users/z523/Library/Application Support/Codex/Crashpad` (from the ChatGPT_Mac process). So ChatGPT.app uses `Application Support/Codex`.\n- `com.openai.codex` in Application Support — could be from the official Codex desktop app (which might exist separately) or from the CLI.\n\nHmm, and ~/.codex is the Codex home directory used by both CLI and desktop app.\n\nWait, actually — I need to reconsider. Is there actually a separate official \"Codex\" desktop app installed? Let me check /Applications more carefully — I grepped for \"codex|openai\" and got: \"Codex++ 管理工具.app\", \"Codex++.app\". No official \"Codex.app\". But ChatGPT.app matched \"openai\"? Actually my grep was `grep -i -E \"codex|openai\"` and output showed only Codex++ entries and... hmm wait, the first command output only showed `Codex++ 管理工具.app` and `Codex++.app` — ChatGPT.app didn't match the grep (no \"openai\" in \"ChatGPT.app\"). But pgrep showed ChatGPT.app is running with Codex Framework.\n\nSo on this machine:\n- ChatGPT.app (official ChatGPT desktop) — contains Codex Framework (used for its coding features?)\n- Codex++.app (third-party wrapper)\n- No standalone Codex CLI\n\nThe user chose \"官方 Codex 桌面/内核\" = \"卸载 ChatGPT.app 相关的 Codex 内核部分重装\" (my option description said uninstall the Codex kernel part related to ChatGPT.app and reinstall).\n\nHmm, but here's the problem: **the Codex Framework inside ChatGPT.app is not independently uninstallable** — it's bundled inside the ChatGPT app bundle. You can't remove \"just the Codex kernel\" from ChatGPT.app without breaking the app. Options:\n\n1. Uninstall the whole ChatGPT.app and reinstall it (gets latest Codex Framework bundled).\n2. If they mean the official Codex desktop app — it doesn't appear to be installed standalone.\n\nWait, but maybe I should double check whether there IS a separate official Codex app somewhere. `com.openai.codex` in Application Support is suspicious. Let me look inside it. Also check ~/Applications (earlier grep showed nothing there). Let me check:\n- `ls ~/Library/Application Support/com.openai.codex`\n- `ls ~/Library/Application Support/Codex`\n- mdfind for Codex.app: `mdfind \"kMDItemFSName == 'Codex.app'\"` or check /Applications full listing.\n\nActually — wait. Reconsider. What is Codex++ exactly? 20MB app, bundle com.bigpizzav3.codexplusplus. It's a third-party app. There's a known thing: \"Codex++\" might be a launcher/patcher for the official Codex... Actually, hmm, there's a known third-party project \"CodexPlus\". Not important — what matters is what the user wants.\n\nThe user's mental model: \"本地codex\" broken somehow, wants clean reinstall. They picked \"官方 Codex 桌面/内核\" and \"全部清空\" (wipe ~/.codex too).\n\nPlan:\n1. First verify what official Codex desktop exists: check if there's a Codex.app anywhere, check com.openai.codex contents.\n2. The realistic reinstall path for \"官方 Codex 桌面/内核\":\n   - The Codex Framework lives inside ChatGPT.app → reinstall ChatGPT.app (download fresh from openai.com) — that updates the bundled Codex Framework.\n   - `~/Library/Application Support/Codex`, `com.openai.codex`, and `~/.codex` are the data dirs.\n3. Wipe: ~/.codex (they approved), plus Application Support/Codex + com.openai.codex? They said \"全部清空\" for ~/.codex specifically (the question was about ~/.codex). For the app data dirs, a \"clean reinstall\" of the kernel would also include those. But careful — ChatGPT.app's Application Support/Codex contains its browser/codex state.\n\nHold on. Before I go deleting things, I should be careful here. This is destructive (user did authorize: \"全部清空\" + they asked me to handle it). But there are important caveats:\n\n1. **Killing running processes**: ChatGPT.app is running (crashpad handlers at least), Codex++ 管理工具 is running, chrome extension host from ~/.codex/plugins is running. Deleting ~/.codex while codex processes run will cause errors/partial recreation. Need to quit them first.\n\n2. **~/.codex wipe**: loses login (need re-login), sessions, plugins. User approved.\n\n3. **The plugin marketplace clone in progress** (ponytail.git from github.com/DietrichGebert/ponytail) — wiping ~/.codex kills that; fine.\n\n4. **Codex++ might be what actually launches Codex** — if Codex++ is a wrapper around the official core... The official \"core\" here — hmm wait. Actually now I realize: maybe the official Codex desktop app IS what's providing ~/.codex activity, launched BY Codex++? Or the user uses ChatGPT.app's codex?\n\nHmm, let me also reconsider: could there be an official Codex.app in /Applications that my grep missed? My grep pattern `grep -i -E \"codex|openai\"` on `ls /Applications` — \"Codex.app\" would match \"codex\". Output showed only Codex++ apps. So no standalone Codex.app. \n\nBut wait — what about the App Library paths: `~/Applications`? Also nothing. So the \"official Codex desktop\" exists only as the embedded framework in ChatGPT.app.\n\nHmm hold on, actually let me reconsider once more. There's another possibility: the official Codex desktop app in 2026 might be distributed as \"Codex\" inside ChatGPT.app (the ChatGPT desktop app has a Codex section), OR as a separate \"Codex.app\". On this machine: only embedded. OK.\n\nSo the honest answer to the user: **官方 Codex 桌面/内核不是一个独立 App，它内嵌在 ChatGPT.app 里**，所以\"卸载重装\"意味着重装整个 ChatGPT.app。另外，真正日常在跑的 Codex 数据在 ~/.codex。\n\nWait, actually — one more thought. Where does ~/.codex's active usage come from? The running process `/Users/z523/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/chrome/latest/extension-host/macos/arm64/ChatGPT for Chrome` — \"openai-bundled\" plugin cache under ~/.codex. This is the official Codex app-server / ChatGPT app's codex infrastructure. The chrome-native-hosts-v2.json in ~/.codex registers native messaging hosts for Chrome extension. So ChatGPT.app (or its Codex part) actively uses ~/.codex. Also Codex++ likely uses the same ~/.codex home.\n\nSo the clean-reinstall plan for \"官方 Codex 桌面/内核\":\n\nOption A (what they asked): \n1. Quit ChatGPT.app, Codex++, Codex++ 管理工具, and the codex/extension-host processes.\n2. Reinstall ChatGPT.app fresh (delete app, download latest from OpenAI) — brings latest Codex Framework.\n3. Wipe ~/.codex + ~/Library/Application Support/Codex + com.openai.codex (app-level state) — full clean.\n4. User re-logs into ChatGPT.app / codex.\n\nBut hmm — deleting ChatGPT.app: does the user have the installer? I can download from https://chatgpt.com/download or it might be auto-updating. Downloading ChatGPT.dmg requires network; doable via curl. Actually, is that too far? The user picked \"官方 Codex 桌面/内核\" whose description I wrote as \"卸载 ChatGPT.app 相关的 Codex 内核部分重装\". So yes they expect that.\n\nBut wait — I should double-check whether ChatGPT.app is actually the thing to reinstall, vs. Codex++ being the wrapper that installed its own codex core somewhere. Codex++ 管理工具 — \"manager tool\" — 20MB Codex++.app. Where would Codex++'s core live? Maybe in ~/.codex too, or Application Support/CodexDreamSkinStudio (that's \"codex-dream-skin-studio\" — a skin studio plugin for codex? matches ~/.codex/codex-dream-skin-studio dir).\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Honestly the machine has a tangle: Codex++ (third-party) + ChatGPT.app (official) + ~/.codex shared. \n\nPractical approach: do what's asked in a safe order, and verify at each step:\n\nStep 0: Confirm plan with user? They already approved target + wipe. But there's a nuance they should know: the Codex kernel is inside ChatGPT.app, so \"reinstall kernel\" = reinstall ChatGPT.app entirely. And wiping ~/.codex logs out everything including Codex++ (shared home). I think I should just briefly state this and proceed — they said \"能帮我处理一下吗\" (handle it for me). Asking again would be annoying. But deleting ChatGPT.app + wiping data is destructive-ish; they've authorized \"卸载重装 + 全部清空\". The data being wiped: codex config/sessions/plugins (approved explicitly), app data dirs (implied by \"clean reinstall\"... hmm, my wipe question was specifically about ~/.codex. Application Support/Codex + com.openai.codex — deleting those also removes ChatGPT desktop's codex browser state. For a \"彻底干净重来\" intent, include them. But cautious: I'll back up nothing? User said wipe. \n\nHmm wait, one thing to reconsider: is ChatGPT.app itself something the user wants to keep? They use ChatGPT desktop for chat presumably. Reinstalling it = lose nothing except needing re-login (auth stored in keychain or app data). If I delete Application Support/Codex only (not all ChatGPT data), ChatGPT chat history is in cloud + `Application Support/com.openai.chat` maybe. Careful to only touch Codex-specific dirs, not the whole ChatGPT app data.\n\nPlan:\n1. Quit processes: ChatGPT.app (PID 27236/27239 are crashpad helpers — the main app is running), Codex++ 管理工具 (23208), extension host (1820), and any \"Codex\" app-server processes. Use osascript to quit gracefully: `osascript -e 'quit app \"ChatGPT\"' -e 'quit app \"Codex++ 管理工具\"' -e 'quit app \"Codex++\"'` then pkill leftovers.\n2. Delete ~/.codex (user approved wipe).\n3. Delete ~/Library/Application Support/Codex and com.openai.codex (codex kernel state).\n\nHmm, actually about com.openai.codex — let me look at what it is first before deleting. Let me peek at its contents before wiping.\n\n4. Delete ChatGPT.app → download latest → install to /Applications. Download URL: https://chatgpt.com/download → redirects to dmg. `curl -L -o /tmp/ChatGPT.dmg \"https://persistent.oaistatic.com/...\"`? The official download link: https://chatgpt.com/download redirects. Let me try `curl -sIL https://chatgpt.com/download` to find it. macOS Tahoe — ChatGPT may also be on Mac App Store; direct dmg download exists. If download fails, I can leave a note for user to download manually.\n\nWait — actually, hmm. Deleting the whole ChatGPT.app is a bigger action than \"reinstall codex kernel\". Is there a lighter path? The Codex Framework gets updated when ChatGPT.app auto-updates. A \"clean reinstall of ChatGPT.app\" does achieve a fresh Codex Framework. I'll do it but tell the user that's what it means. Also they might need the login again (ChatGPT app login is typically in Keychain — preserved).\n\n5. After reinstall: launch ChatGPT.app, verify Codex Framework version present. ~/.codex will be recreated on first run; user re-logs in (codex login via ChatGPT account).\n\nAlso — Codex++ depends on ~/.codex? After wipe, Codex++ would also need re-setup. User approved full wipe, fine. I'll mention it.\n\nOrder of operations matters: quit apps → verify processes gone → delete data dirs → delete app → download+install → relaunch.\n\nRisk check: \n- Deleting ChatGPT.app while it's running — must quit first. \n- Download ~size of ChatGPT.dmg (~few hundred MB) — takes a bit; run in background with curl.\n- Keychain entries remain → re-login may be automatic.\n\nAlso the earlier weird process: git clone of DietrichGebert/ponytail.git into ~/.codex/plugins/.marketplace-plugin-source-staging — wiping ~/.codex while that clone runs → clone fails, fine. But should I mention this to user? It's a plugin marketplace install (probably triggered by Codex++ or user earlier). Killing it is part of cleanup. I'll note it briefly — actually, hmm, it's a bit odd (random GitHub repo). I'll mention it in one line in the report so they know it got cancelled, in case they were installing a plugin on purpose.\n\nWait, actually, one more consideration on \"wiping ~/.codex\": there's a `backups` dir inside. User approved. Fine.\n\nAlso AGENTS.md in ~/.codex — that's the user's global agent instructions file! If they have custom AGENTS.md there, wiping loses it. It's 1247 bytes. Hmm — user said 全部清空. But AGENTS.md is user-authored content, not app-generated. Prudent: back it up to Desktop or /tmp before wipe? A good assistant would preserve user-authored files. Let me back up ~/.codex/AGENTS.md and config-ortigin.toml (the \"ortigin\" typo file, 4.7KB — looks like an old config backup the user made manually? \"config-ortigin.toml\" = config-origin typo'd — likely user's manual backup). Hmm — user said wipe everything. But these are user-created files inside. To be safe and kind: move AGENTS.md + config-ortigin.toml to a backup folder on Desktop (or ~/Desktop/codex-backup) and tell them. Small, reversible, respects intent (clean reinstall) without destroying user-authored content. Actually — hmm, is that overstepping? No: deleting is irreversible; keeping a tiny backup of user-authored files is the careful choice, and I'll tell them where it is. Actually, let me reconsider — user explicitly chose \"全部清空...彻底干净\". They want clean. AGENTS.md might even be the thing causing their problem (bad global config?). I'll still back up the two user-authored files to Desktop — costs nothing, protects against regret. I'll state it clearly.\n\nHmm, also auth.json (77 bytes) — tiny, that's login token; wipe per approval.\n\nLet me also check: is ChatGPT.app currently the running one from /Applications? Yes. Any pending download? Fine.\n\nAlso should I check whether ChatGPT.app can be redownloaded — check https://chatgpt.com/download. I'll curl -I to see redirect target.\n\nLet me also confirm disk path sizes: ~/.codex is 722264 blocks? No wait, `total 722264` in ls -la is 512-byte blocks for the dir listing = ~350MB? Actually that's the total of listed entries at top level (mostly .tmp probably). Fine.\n\nExecution plan (commands):\n\nPhase 1 — quit apps:\n```\nosascript -e 'tell application \"ChatGPT\" to quit' 2>/dev/null; osascript -e 'tell application \"Codex++ 管理工具\" to quit' 2>/dev/null; osascript -e 'tell application \"Codex++\" to quit' 2>/dev/null; sleep 2; pgrep -fl -i -E \"codex|chatgpt\" | head\n```\nThen pkill stubborn ones: `pkill -f \"Codex Framework\"` etc. Careful — pkill -i codex would kill... the extension host 1820 and crashpads. Let me do targeted pkill.\n\nPhase 2 — backup user-authored files:\n```\nmkdir -p ~/Desktop/codex-backup-20260930 && cp ~/.codex/AGENTS.md ~/.codex/config-ortigin.toml ~/Desktop/codex-backup-20260930/\n```\nWait, the date — env says 2026-09-30 but files show Oct 8?? The file timestamps say Oct 8 10:59 — that's AFTER today's date per env. Weird clock? Whatever, I'll name it without date: ~/Desktop/codex-backup.\n\nHmm wait, actually — hold on. Let me recheck: is ChatGPT.app really what user wants to delete? They selected \"官方 Codex 桌面/内核\" whose label description: \"卸载 ChatGPT.app 相关的 Codex 内核部分重装\". Yes.\n\nBut actually — WAIT. One more careful thought. ChatGPT.app version 152.0.7977.83 — that's the ChatGPT macOS app. If I delete ChatGPT.app and redownload, user needs to re-login ChatGPT (keychain may preserve). And the \"Codex 内核\" fresh = latest version. OK.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: can I even download ChatGPT.dmg without user interaction? `https://chatgpt.com/download` — let me check with curl. If it 403s or needs JS, fallback: tell user to download from Mac App Store / website, while I still do the wipe + quit + reinstall prep. Alternatively check if `brew install --cask chatgpt` works — brew is present (/opt/homebrew). brew cask \"chatgpt\" exists (official ChatGPT app). That's a clean path: `brew install --cask chatgpt`. But if the user's ChatGPT.app was installed via App Store, brew cask install would be a duplicate... The app is in /Applications (not /Applications/Setapp etc.). App Store apps can be identified by _MASReceipt. Check: `ls /Applications/ChatGPT.app/Contents/_MASReceipt` — if exists, it's App Store → reinstall should go through App Store (I ca